```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: ML researchers and evaluators running n-gram decontamination (GPT-3/Llama standard) on QA/reasoning benchmarks where item length correlates with difficulty (e.g., GSM8K, QuALITY). They need to know post-decontamination accuracy is inflated by 0.9-1.7 points at realistic settings (k=8/13, med coverage, alpha=0.3-0.6) due to length-biased removal of harder items. For MMLU-style MCQ where length is dominated by option boilerplate (alpha~0), the finding provides actionable confirmation that decontamination is safe (Delta~0, CI covers 0).
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Real win, not microbenchmark artifact. The structural anchor holds unconditionally (removal monotone in length across all 9 (c,k) cells, survivor mean-length 24.7->18.3 tokens, empirical removal matches union-bound P(remove|L)=1-(1-q)^(L-k+1) with MAE=0.012). The alpha=0 control passes (max|Delta@a0|=0.00109 vs headline up to +0.091), proving inflation is coupling-driven, not artifact. Effect persists at production k in {8,13} (Delta=+0.016/+0.017, CI excludes 0) and across coverage levels. Magnitude 0.9-1.7pts is operationally meaningful for competitive leaderboard rankings, though smaller than the 8-14pt contamination gains reported by Llama3 (Dubey 2407.21783). For low-alpha benchmarks, the value is the negative result confirming safety.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Diagnostic finding, not a drop-in tool—no alternative decontamination method proposed; community inertia around GPT-3 (Brown 2005.14165) / Llama3 (Dubey 2407.21783) 8/13-gram standard; requires per-benchmark length-difficulty correlation measurement (alpha) overhead; effect size (0.9-1.7pts) may not change decisions for teams already managing larger contamination uncertainties (8-14pts); Bordt 2410.03249 observed "significant filtering" but called removal benign, indicating community may undervalue the bias]
RATIONALE: EXP-0059 evidence demonstrates the decontamination filter is a length-biased sampler by construction (union-bound), with the alpha=0 control proving the score inflation is real and coupling-gated, not a measurement artifact. The bias persists at production k values and realistic corpus coverage, with Delta growing monotone in alpha (0.0 to +0.0246) and coverage (low/med/high: +0.004/+0.017/+0.064). Closest prior work Bordt 2410.03249 applied the filter and noted significant removal but treated it as benign collateral; this work proves the collateral is not benign for length-correlated benchmarks. Actionable guidance: measure your benchmark's length-difficulty slope; if alpha~0 (MMLU-like), n-gram decontamination is safe; if alpha>0 (GSM8K-like), expect 1-2pt inflation and adjust claims or consider alternatives. The finding is methodologically valuable but adoption requires community standard change without a provided replacement.
```
